<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Didessa River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Didessa is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and a major tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a>, the Blue Nile. In the 19th century its upper valley was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> kingdoms of Gomma, Gumma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmu">Limmu</a>-Ennarea, whose wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, gold and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/didessa-river.jpg" alt="Didessa River" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Braxmeier &amp; Peter in s, Location map:Sting · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbay_OSM.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Didessa, Dhedheessa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, rises in the mountains of Gomma and flows north-west to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a> where that river has curved to its southernmost point before turning north. Its drainage area of about 25,800 square kilometres covers parts of the Benishangul-Gumuz Region and the West Welega Zone of the Oromia Region. Its right-bank tributaries include the Enareya, Aet, Wama and Angar, and its chief left-bank tributary is the Dobana. Alexander Bulatovich, who explored the river in the mid-1890s and questioned people living along it, held that below the Angar junction it is free of rapids and could be navigable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early 20th-century explorer Herbert Weld Blundell thought the name Didessa had replaced a much older one, finding no use of it before 1861, when d'Abbadie made inquiries in Western Shoa. At his visit in 1905 the Handak forest on the right bank was a favourite haunt of elephants, and at the meeting of the Didessa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a> gold-washing began in earnest, continuing along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and down the Dabus; he described quartz pebbles and outcrops strewn over some 15 miles, from which gold washed into the streams.</p><p>Although people had lived in the area for a very long time, the river's course from near Nekemte to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a> was apparently first traced only in 1935, by Dunlop and Taylor. Dejazmach Habte Maryam, the local official in Nekemte, told them that nobody, as far as he knew, had followed it to the junction, warning of the lack of paths, thick bamboo forest, fever and steep mountains, and calling the country fit only for monkeys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 14 June 2021 construction of the Anger Irrigation Dam on the Didessa was launched by Shimelis Abdisa, President of the Oromia regional state, and Sileshi Bekele, Minister of Water, Irrigation and Energy, with three years planned for the work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Didessa River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gomma emerged around 1800 in the upper <b>Didessa</b> and Gibe valleys, one of the monarchies formed as Oromo settlers of the region replaced the egalitarian gadaa system with hereditary kingship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gomma">Kingdom of Gomma</a></li><li><blockquote>Gumma emerged around 1810 west of the <b>Didessa</b>, as Oromo groups of the region formed hereditary monarchies.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gumma">Kingdom of Gumma</a></li></ul></section>
